From: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: stmmac: dwmac-ipq5018: advertise only 2500BASEX on a
 port pinned to it

GMAC1 can run SGMII, 1000BASEX or 2500BASEX through the UNIPHY PCS, and
advertising all three is right for most boards. It is wrong for a board whose
external PHY does rate matching: an RTL8221B offered both 2500BASEX and SGMII
picks the 2500BASEX/SGMII auto-switch mode, which needs the UNIPHY switched to
SGMII at runtime whenever the copper side negotiates 1G. That switch is
unreliable here - a 1G peer links, and then no stable data flows.

Advertise only 2500BASEX when, and only when, device tree pins the port at
2500base-x. The PHY then keeps its SerDes fixed and pause-rate-adapts slower
copper speeds internally, which is what it is built to do. HW-verified on an
IPQ5018 board (QDM530) against a native 2.5G peer and a 1G peer, including
hot-plug between them.

Keyed off plat->phy_interface rather than applied to every PCS-backed GMAC1:
the earlier version of this took SGMII and 1000BASEX away from any board with a
PCS, which removes capability from boards that have no rate-matching PHY and
need the UNIPHY switching to work. Those keep the full set.

To be precise about what the key means, since it is the weak point of this
patch: phy-mode = "2500base-x" states the configured interface, it does not
declare that runtime interface switching is forbidden, and phylink will let a
PHY change interface when the MAC advertises more than one. Reading it as a
restriction is this driver's policy, chosen because the switch is unreliable on
this IP. It therefore still affects any board using this driver in that mode,
including one that wanted dynamic switching to work - narrower than before, but
not free. Upstream this needs either a justification covering every 2500BASE-X
configuration of this driver, or a narrower key than the interface mode.

Signed-off-by: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-ipq5018.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-ipq5018.c
@@ -115,7 +115,39 @@
 	 * - GMAC0 supports SGMII and is wired to the SoC's internal GE PHY
 	 * - GMAC1 supports SGMII, 1000BaseX, and 2500BaseX, configurable by
 	 *   the UNIPHY PCS
+	 *
+	 * One exception. When device tree configures this port as 2500base-x,
+	 * this driver treats that as "stay there" and advertises only that
+	 * mode. Note that is a policy of this driver, not something the DT
+	 * property means on its own: phy-mode states the configured interface,
+	 * and phylink is perfectly willing to let a PHY change interface at
+	 * runtime when the MAC advertises more than one.
+	 *
+	 * The reason to refuse that here: a rate-matching PHY (an RTL8221B, say) then
+	 * keeps its SerDes fixed at 2500BASEX and pause-rate-adapts slower
+	 * copper speeds internally. Offer it SGMII as well and it picks the
+	 * 2500BASEX/SGMII auto-switch mode instead, which needs the UNIPHY
+	 * switched to SGMII at runtime for a 1G link; that switch is unreliable
+	 * and leaves 1G peers linked but without stable data flow.
+	 *
+	 * Keyed off the device-tree mode rather than applied to every
+	 * PCS-backed GMAC1, so a board that does not pin the mode keeps the
+	 * full set of interfaces.
 	 */
+	if (priv->hw->phylink_pcs &&
+	    priv->plat->phy_interface == PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_2500BASEX) {
+		__set_bit(PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_2500BASEX, interfaces);
+
+		/*
+		 * Synopsys DWMAC IP version 3.7 is limited to 1 Gpbs.
+		 * This vendor specific implementation supports 2.5 Gbps, so override
+		 * the default mac link capabilities.
+		 */
+		mac->link.caps |= MAC_2500FD;
+
+		return;
+	}
+
 	__set_bit(PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_SGMII, interfaces);
 	if (priv->hw->phylink_pcs) {
 		__set_bit(PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_1000BASEX, interfaces);
